---
id: PY-005
type: rule
family: python-reliability
title: 'wildcard import (from x import *)'
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 3

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.py]
allowlist: ['**/test_*.py', '**/*_test.py', '**/tests/**', '**/conftest.py', '**/explain/**', '**/__init__.py']

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '^\s*from\s+[.\w]+\s+import\s+\*'

related: []
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

`from module import *` pulls an unknown set of names into this namespace,
shadowing locals unpredictably and defeating static analysis of what is used.
Import the names you actually use explicitly: `from module import PORT, HOST`.

`__init__.py` is allowlisted: re-exporting a package's public API with
`from .submodule import *` is the one conventional use of the wildcard, so
flagging it on by default would just be day-one noise.
